Title: "Overview"
Id: "7ad33634-c9f0-482e-80da-39d9d7d70c23"

Text: "Type statements use `is`; predicate-like checks use `is?`.

```mlg-fragment
X is \set
f is \function:on{A}:to{B}
A, B is \set
x is? \set
```

Builtin types start with two backslashes:

- `\\type` — the type of commands declared by top-level `Declares:` items
- `\\statement`, `\\expression`, and `\\specification` — the types of those
  formulation categories
- `\\anything` — a universal unstructured value; it establishes no more
  specific type information

`\\abstract` is not a type; it is the special abstract target of a `:->` or
`:<->:` capability. The former type name `\\opaque` is unsupported. The right
side of `is` may also be a structural type literal, which uses a complete `_`
spec literal at every leaf:

```mlg-fragment
(x, y) is (_ is \natural, _ \"in\" \reals)
{x : ...} is {_ is \natural : ...}
f is (_ is \natural) -> (_ \"in\" \naturals)
f is (_ is \natural, _ \"in\" \reals) -> (_ is \real)
```

A raw nominal tuple such as `(\natural, \real)` is not a type literal. The
`->` form preserves its input arity and has exactly one output specification."
Id: "2f73683b-6420-4fe6-8196-3ce83be64464"
